  "answer_text": "**Medi-Cal** is California\u2019s Medicaid program, which provides free or low-cost health coverage to eligible California residents, including low-income individuals, families, seniors, people with disabilities, pregnant individuals, and foster youth. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Key Medi-Cal Changes\nSeveral critical changes took effect, affecting eligibility, assets, and renewals:\n\n*   **Reinstated Asset Limits:** For individuals qualifying based on age (65 and older), disability, or long-term care needs, California has reinstated an asset check. \n    *   **The Limit:** You can own up to **$130,000** in countable assets as an individual, plus **$65,000** for each additional family member. \n    *   **What Counts:** Cash, bank accounts, secondary homes, or additional vehicles.\n    *   **What is Exempt:** Your primary home, one vehicle, household items, and certain retirement accounts.\n*   **Immigration-Related Enrollment Freeze:** Adults (ages 19 and older) who are undocumented and not pregnant are no longer eligible to newly enroll in full-scope, state-funded Medi-Cal. \n    *   *Note:* If you were **already enrolled** in full-scope Medi-Cal before this freeze, you will stay covered as long as you complete your renewals on time and meet standard eligibility rules. \n    *   Undocumented children (0\u201318) and pregnant individuals remain eligible to enroll in full-scope coverage regardless of immigration status.\n*   **Upcoming 6-Month Renewals (Starting January 1, 2027):** Adults ages 19 to 64 will transition to renewing their Medi-Cal coverage every six months rather than once a year.\n\n---\n\n### 2. General Eligibility Rules\nTo qualify for Medi-Cal, you must be a resident of California and meet specific income criteria, which is tied to the Federal Poverty Level (FPL).\n*   **Income Limit:** Most adults qualify if their household income is **at or below 138% of the Federal Poverty Level**.\n*   **Special Categories:** Income thresholds may be higher for pregnant individuals, children, and those with specific disabilities. \n\n---\n\n### 3. What Medi-Cal Covers\nMedi-Cal offers comprehensive, \"full-scope\" benefits that cover standard essential health services, usually with $0 copays or premiums:\n*   **Medical Care:** Routine doctor visits, immunizations, preventive care, and specialist appointments.\n*   **Hospital Care:** Emergency room visits, outpatient care, and inpatient hospital stays.\n*   **Prescriptions:** Covered prescription drugs.\n*   **Mental Health:** Therapy, counseling, and substance use disorder treatment.\n*   **Dental Care:** Dental checkups, cleanings, x-rays, fillings, and crowns through Denti-Cal.\n*   **Vision Care:** Routine eye exams and coverage for eyeglasses.\n*   **Transportation:** Non-emergency medical transportation to and from appointments.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Apply\nYou can apply for Medi-Cal year-round (there is no specific open-enrollment period for Medi-Cal). \n\n*   **Online (Fastest):** Apply via [BenefitsCal.com](https://benefitscal.com/) or [CoveredCA.com](https://www.coveredca.com/). The portal will automatically assess whether you qualify for Medi-Cal or a subsidized Covered California plan.\n*   **By Phone:** Call Covered California at **1-800-300-1506** or contact your local county social services office.\n*   **In Person:** Visit your county\u2019s social services office to fill out an application in person.\n*   **By Mail:** Download, print, and mail the Single Streamlined Application to your local county health office.\n\n**What you will need to apply:** Proof of identity (ID/driver\u2019s license), income verification (paystubs, W-2s, tax returns), and, if applicable, asset verification (bank statements) and citizenship/immigration documents.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Keeping Your Medi-Cal Active\nIf you are already enrolled, **watch your mail closely**. When your county sends your annual renewal packet, you must complete and return it by the due date. Missing a renewal deadline can cause your coverage to lapse. If you have moved, be sure to update your address immediately with your county social services office or online via BenefitsCal.",
